2186045417] [17592186192696 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192696 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Principal Component Given a 2x2 Covariance Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192696 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192696 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "40fb56dd-7e99-5cb6-a3f6-f9ddd630c730" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192865 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192865 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192865 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192865 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192865 #edb/ref 1045 "Computing Principal Components" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192865 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192865 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192865 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192865 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "757509db-ea62-5c97-af42-cffc3637196f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192964 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186189670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192964 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192964 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192964 #edb/ref 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $10$ and $5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192964 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d35740ea-ab16-5dbd-b830-35948c9ebd0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192964 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193502 #edb/ref 1061 36991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193502 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2774fb5d-55a7-5428-8581-814bde185950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193502 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193523 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193523 #edb/ref 1061 39395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193523 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c43bb8da-171f-50ea-8f50-5beb61c94790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193523 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193525 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193525 #edb/ref 1061 39400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193525 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b9d675a-327f-591d-9bc2-fa8d6b6ff934" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193525 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193823 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the principal components of a standardized observation matrix $X$ are the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$ If \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1,$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nFor example, suppose that the covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is \n\n![](images/e4/e40cd1a0df89ba171b921ab971e8eee25e284265df13b7e031ea32fc28d2c27b.svg)\n\nIt's easy to show that the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=10, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the first principal component of the data, we need the unit eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=10.$ \n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, seeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector \n\n![](images/c7/c7050bbb395b939b1b0559b3e7515c4be4f572aed61090f1a4489e126763240f.svg) \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n![](images/7b/7b0a41deb385aa2471cae701c9f87938085256879a8d834535f8ec0e3f431f27.svg) \n\nTherefore, the $1$st principal component of the data is ![](images/94/9490f2e5105592fa3976ae506cbdf64d70c3372d1cc9295eaa6c6580a4ac75aa.svg)\n\n**Note:** The opposite vector ![](images/e2/e2b366cfb8cb5b708eb5ba443dfc61c05b498d4eb737b375c8ebc86b96e8eea6.svg) also works in the example above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193823 #edb/ref 1065 6507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193823 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid