5417] [17592186533994 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533999 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533999 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533999 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533999 #edb/ref 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1$ and $6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533999 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533999 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534045 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534045 #edb/ref 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $2,$ and $9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a946e21d-5a37-5585-a234-01e6acc34eca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534045 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534047 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534047 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534047 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534047 #edb/ref 1071 "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the third principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $8,$ $5,$ and $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "acbab80b-b003-5343-8102-3dfe5d2664d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534047 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534141 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534141 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a3410547-abc5-50e3-b02f-0dc8ed29d6e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534141 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534144 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534144 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b50ea736-e70c-587d-b358-26135009e6d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534144 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186533999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534149 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534149 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cdf898f6-45d1-58e0-a005-fd3296906